          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reproduction: How Life Continues"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.953,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 225"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.099,
            0.728,
            0.118
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Birth control methods include condoms, pills, IUDs (Copper-T), etc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.12,
            0.753,
            0.189
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If fertilisation occurs, the zygote develops into an embryo and pregnancy begins. In human females, pregnancy usually lasts about nine months, during which the mother’s body undergoes many changes to support the baby’s growth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.13,
            0.191,
            0.753,
            0.245
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It is important for the mother to have proper nutrition, check-ups and rest, avoid harmful substances for the baby’s healthy growth and support her own body during pregnancy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.295,
            0.279,
            0.372
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.174,
                0.295,
                0.279,
                0.372
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.347,
            0.328,
            0.654,
            0.351
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Revise, Reflect, Refine"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.379,
            0.775,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. A flower’s anthers are removed before it matures. Later, pollen from another plant of the same species is dusted onto its stigma and seeds are produced. Which process has been ensured here? (i) Self-pollination (ii) Cross-pollination (iii) Fertilisation (iv) Tissue culture"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.474,
            0.773,
            0.551
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Arrange the following stages of sexual reproduction in plants in the correct order: (i) Pollen germination on stigma (ii) Fertilisation (iii) Pollination (iv) Formation of zygote"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.553,
            0.77,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Assertion (A): The zygote formed after fertilisation immediately attaches to the uterus wall. Reason (R): The uterus wall is always prepared to receive the zygote. (i) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A. (ii) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A. (iii) A is true, but R is false. (iv) A is false, but R is true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.682,
            0.774,
            0.717
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Why does asexual reproduction produce offsprings that are genetically identical to the parent?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.72,
            0.663,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Explain why the menstrual cycle stops during pregnancy."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.74,
            0.774,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Why are flowers that bloom at night white or light in colour as compared to flowers that bloom during the day?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.778,
            0.774,
            0.813
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Why do vegetatively propagated plants tend to be more vulnerable to diseases than sexually reproduced plants?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.121,
            0.815,
            0.774,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. If all flowers in a type of plant were only capable of self-pollination, how would it affect the genetic diversity over several generations? Explain."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.122,
            0.87,
            0.775,
            0.923
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "9. A farmer wants to produce a large number of genetically identical plants quickly. Suggest suitable reproduction methods and explain why they are effective."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
